  "live_records": {
    "q-88209": {
      "dom_id": "step-q88209",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
