    "q-88688": {
      "dom_id": "step-q88688",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}3{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{7}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{7} \\\\  & =3(-{x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{7} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}3{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={e}^{2x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{2}{x-2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =\\frac{2}{(-x)-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-x-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x+2} \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
