    "q-316106": {
      "dom_id": "step-q316106",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}sin\\,({x}^{7})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{7})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{7})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =sin\\,((-x)^{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{7})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{7})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
